#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2cfbdf5-7a12-50c7-8aeb-12200c63215d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122795} {:answer/id #uuid "ac79bc48-f741-58e5-a934-70d76f745277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122796} {:answer/id #uuid "ee578321-16f6-590e-9916-36b7f8c167ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122797} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2722a5-497c-559c-a53f-aa17cc466294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122798} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd378c3-b003-5741-a6e9-3e2b15d6191b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2cfbdf5-7a12-50c7-8aeb-12200c63215d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122795} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9b4e15-e51f-5af1-83c5-1301e70ae602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3186ab9d-e1b7-5f08-9515-496d883e1032" :question/math-academy-id "q-37270" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$\n\n$$\nB={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff814f1f-861a-53df-8eab-c7d7c9933124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351619} {:answer/id #uuid "00741376-4eeb-58cf-a42c-c862621f7b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351620} {:answer/id #uuid "9819ff40-49cf-5bf4-b9a0-6a1a026ef323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351621} {:answer/id #uuid "0d171951-79ba-52c1-834d-3c8a9583174d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351622} {:answer/id #uuid "39699e75-5337-509f-81fc-8edf04e08698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d171951-79ba-52c1-834d-3c8a9583174d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351622} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb1a21af-91d7-592e-8dd1-00a6de64dd10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37a50d07-181f-50e1-b1a0-fc697fe75537" :question/math-academy-id "q-37625" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{69}$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{69} & =P{D}^{69}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{69}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{69} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{69}\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
